# Dream Sequence Memory Fabric

Dreaming is not a repo-local search report.

A real dream sequence should inspect the whole system at the right scope, then pull near-term activity, far-term memory, current outputs, and repeated patterns into a reviewable set of dreams. It should be curious enough to find novel work, but bounded enough that private data, customer data, secrets, and raw transcripts do not leak into public artifacts.

## Goal

Build Dreaming as a dynamic workflow that turns the JoelClaw network into a source-backed learning loop for the kernel/package system.

The workflow only counts if it is real and provably run in Cloudflare. A local harness, static branch table, hand-written `if`/`else`/`switch` dispatcher, canned phase list, or deterministic script that merely labels itself "dynamic" fails the definition of done. The run must include the planner prompt/transcript, generated workflow machine config, generated TypeScript source, generated harness source, artifact hashes, Cloudflare run id/event receipts, capability lease receipts, and verifier proof that Cloudflare executed the generated machine artifacts.

The workflow should provision scoped capabilities to inspect and repair the memory fabric across `blaine`, `panda`, `flagg`, Cloudflare, and local machines. It first inventories transcript stores, Brain roots, docs/pdf-brain, Cloudflare artifacts, workflow outputs, GitHub/Slack/Linear/support surfaces, and org/project graphs. It then checks ingest health, backfills missing derived indexes from authoritative sources, and fixes capture paths so backfill becomes recovery, not normal operation.

The dream itself runs T-shaped: wide across the whole timeline and system graph, then deep into selected seams with hydrated receipts. It must cover near-term activity, long-term memory, repeated corrections, prior reports, accepted Brain claims, package manifests, workflow receipts, and runtime outputs. A dream is invalid if it only answers "what happened just now" or if Codex/Claude/Cloudflare coverage is false or missing without saying so.

Cloudflare does not get raw credentials or private paths. It leases memory/search/hydration/backfill/report capabilities through a trusted relay. The relay owns raw transcript access, Typesense/docs credentials, org policy, redaction, and source-specific backfills. Cloudflare gets redacted evidence, hashes, freshness, coverage counts, and follow-up links.

The output is not just a report. It is a refinement mechanism: dreams propose kernel memories, package boundaries, workflow-node plugins, access leases, schema changes, report-node improvements, and dynamic workflow patterns. Each accepted dream should leave receipts, update Brain/package artifacts, and feed the next generated workflow so the system gets sharper instead of accumulating more bullshit.

## Diagnosis

The current dream hunt is useful proof that the idea has legs, but it is still too shallow.

Receipts:

